{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean head e82d9fff2d858662f7e3ddc2492c6d5b559d46fa, primary artifact 1e2c6e9ba0289e5592350d3673c45ea3478ab657, citing all six exact-head artifacts: 1e2c6e9b .github/workflows/ci.yml, ef5ac9f8 .github/scripts/verify-preview-clone.sh, f45b510b Makefile, eb88dd80 layout_test.go, c4fff6db public_surface_test.go, and 63849a04 spike.\n\nOne blocker, and it is narrow. Everything else in the design holds and I verified the parts that are claims about behaviour by running them.\n\nThe blocker is the removed forbidden-path list in layout_test.go. It asserted that spike/go.mod, spike/internal, spike/cmd/gs and spike/cmd/gitseq-mcp do not exist, and its comment said why: so future work cannot quietly move shipping code back under the adversarial spike. Nothing at this head asserts that any more. I searched the whole tree at the exact head; the only surviving mentions of those paths are fixture strings inside fold_test.go and a substring check in the report tool, none of which look at the filesystem. The condition I was asked to verify is that the removed tripwires prove nothing unique, and three of these four prove something unique that compilation cannot: compilation proves a path exists, never that one is absent.\n\nOne of the four is now covered indirectly, and I want to give the design credit for that rather than overstate the finding. If spike/go.mod appeared, the spike packages would leave the root module, the single go test -race -json ./... run would stop emitting their cases, and the evidence step would fail. I tested that rather than assuming it: feeding gitseq-report a JSON with the named cases stripped exits 2 and reddens make spike. So spike/go.mod is caught, by a longer route. spike/internal, spike/cmd/gs and spike/cmd/gitseq-mcp are not caught by anything.\n\nThe remedy is cheap and I would not object to either form: keep the four-line forbidden loop, or state in the test comment which of those facts no longer matter and why. What I cannot verify is the comment as it now stands, which says the retained test pins the two layout facts that neither compilation nor the documentation gates prove — that is true of the retained facts and equally true of three facts it dropped.\n\nEverything else I checked passes, and several parts are better than what they replace.\n\nThe one-suite design preserves the Go and race claims. A single go test -race -count=1 -json ./... is a superset of the separate make test and make race lanes for correctness, and -count=1 keeps it uncached. set -o pipefail is what makes this safe, because tee would otherwise swallow a failing exit status, and public_surface_test.go now pins that exact string along with the go test invocation and the make spike SPIKE_TEST_JSON= handoff. Pinning the pipefail line is the detail that turns a fragile pipeline into a checked one.\n\nThe captured-stream and local modes both fail closed, verified by execution. With evidence present, make spike passes and regenerates tracked output. With the named cases stripped from the supplied JSON, it exits 2. That is the property the request asked about and it holds in the direction that matters: absent evidence reddens rather than quietly passing.\n\nThe preview-clone checks survive as claimed: topology assertions on refs/heads/main remain, and the script now runs vet and build rather than test, which is the intended narrowing since the tests already ran once in the main lane.\n\nTiming is credible. I timed the single run rather than repeating a figure: go test -race -count=1 -json ./... completes in 174 seconds here, exit 0, 641 pass events. Against the previous shape, which ran the suite for make test, again for make race, and a third time inside make spike, one run in place of three is a real reduction and the measurement supports the claim.\n\nArchitecture: this touches the build and CI surface only — workflow, Makefile, preview-clone script, two root tests, and the spike reporting tool. No kernel, application fold, projection contract, connector or UI contract changes, and the kernel-application boundary is untouched, so docs/reference/architecture.md need not move.\n\nSecurity: the change concentrates all Go evidence into one stream, which raises the cost of that stream lying. Two things protect it and both are present: pipefail, so a failed suite cannot be masked by the pipe, and fail-closed reporting, so absent cases redden. The residual risk is the blocker above — a silently reduced package set is the one way this stream could shrink without going red, and three of the four guards against that were removed.\n\nSimplification: the deduplication is itself the simplification and it is the right one; running one suite and feeding its stream to the evidence tool is simpler and faster than three runs. I found nothing further worth doing, and I would not merge the forbidden-path check into some other gate — four lines in the test that already owns layout facts is the simplest place for it.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:1e2c6e9ba0289e5592350d3673c45ea3478ab657","head":"e82d9fff2d858662f7e3ddc2492c6d5b559d46fa","verdict":"changes-requested"}}